Assistant: The error seems to be related to module importing and mocking. Let me help fix this by properly setting up the preview component with necessary providers and better mocking.

First, let me create an updated version of the preview file with proper imports and mocking:

<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/Page-ebf1d841.tsx">
  <change>
    <description>Update the preview component with proper imports and mocking</description>
    <content>
import React, { Suspense } from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';

// Create mock components
const MockLegacyPage = () => <div>Mock Legacy Page</div>;
const MockSettingsHeader = ({ children }) => <div>Mock Settings Header {children}</div>;

// Mock modules before importing the component
jest.mock('@calcom/features/ee/teams/pages/team-members-view', () => ({
  __esModule: true,
  default: MockLegacyPage
}));

jest.mock('@calcom/features/settings/appDir/SettingsHeader', () => ({
  __esModule: true,
  default: MockSettingsHeader
}));

jest.mock('app/_utils', () => ({
  __esModule: true,
  _generateMetadata: () => ({}),
  getTranslate: () => Promise.resolve((key) => key),
}));

// Wrap the import in a try-catch to handle potential import errors
let ImportedComponent;
try {
  ImportedComponent = React.lazy(() => import('../../app/(use-page-wrapper)/settings/(settings-layout)/teams/[id]/members/page'));
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Failed to import component:', error);
  ImportedComponent = () => <div>Failed to load component</div>;
}

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({});

  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
      <ImportedComponent />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
</content>
  </change>
</file>

The changes I made include:

1. Added Suspense wrapper around the imported component to handle lazy loading
2. Improved the mocking setup by adding `__esModule: true` to ensure proper ES module compatibility
3. Wrapped the component import in a try-catch block to handle potential import failures gracefully
4. Made the mock components more explicit by declaring them as constants
5. Added proper error handling and fallback UI
6. Used React.lazy for dynamic import to handle the module loading properly

These changes should help resolve the "Failed to fetch dynamically imported module" error by:
1. Providing better error handling for the dynamic import
2. Ensuring proper module mocking
3. Adding Suspense boundary for lazy loading
4. Making the mocks more robust with proper ES module support

The error was likely occurring because the dynamic import wasn't properly handled and the mocks weren't correctly set up as ES modules. The new version should handle these cases better.

