Assistant: Ah, I see the issue. We're trying to use Jest's mocking functionality in a browser environment where Jest isn't available. Let's modify the approach to use simple module mocking without Jest:

<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/Page-ebf1d841.tsx">
  <change>
    <description>Remove Jest mocking and replace with simple mock components</description>
    <content>
import React, { Suspense } from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';

// Mock components and utilities
const MockLegacyPage = () => <div>Mock Legacy Page</div>;
const MockSettingsHeader = ({ children }) => <div>Mock Settings Header {children}</div>;
const mockUtils = {
  _generateMetadata: () => ({}),
  getTranslate: () => Promise.resolve((key) => key),
};

// Mock the modules by overriding the imports
const mockModules = {
  '@calcom/features/ee/teams/pages/team-members-view': MockLegacyPage,
  '@calcom/features/settings/appDir/SettingsHeader': MockSettingsHeader,
  'app/_utils': mockUtils,
};

// Create a wrapped version of the imported component that uses mocks
const ImportedComponent = React.lazy(() => 
  import('../../app/(use-page-wrapper)/settings/(settings-layout)/teams/[id]/members/page')
    .catch(() => ({
      default: () => <div>Failed to load component</div>
    }))
);

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({});

  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
      <ImportedComponent />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
</content>
  </change>
</file>

The changes I made:

1. Removed all Jest-related code (`jest.mock()` calls)
2. Defined mock components and utilities as regular constants
3. Created a `mockModules` object to hold all our mocks (though we're not using it directly, it's there for reference)
4. Simplified the error handling in the lazy import
5. Kept the Suspense wrapper for handling the lazy loading
6. Maintained the basic structure of the component while removing test-specific code

This version should work in a browser environment since it doesn't rely on Jest's mocking system. Instead, it uses simple React components as mocks. The component should now load without the "jest is not defined" error.

If we still need to mock certain modules, we might need to use a different approach or configure the build system to handle module aliasing. But this simpler version should work for most preview cases.

